  "response": "Adjudication begins by acknowledging that texts and strata answer different questions: a scribe records what mattered to a court, whereas a destruction horizon records only that a building burned. Radiocarbon dating of destruction layers supplies a chronology independent of the textual record, and it shows that the Late Bronze Age collapse unfolded across the eastern Mediterranean between roughly 1200 and 1150 BCE. By contrast, dendrochronological sequences fix the onset of the collapse precisely to the year 1177 BCE. That precision cannot be squared with the documentary history of the figure, for a chronological review panel ultimately determined that 1177 BCE is a symbolic marker derived from Egyptian records rather than a tree-ring-fixed date.\n\nWithin the textual record itself, the first task is to establish who is speaking and when. Inscriptions at Medinet Habu record Ramesses III's battles against a coalition known as the Sea Peoples. The Medinet Habu reliefs date the great naval battle against the Sea Peoples to the eighth year of Merneptah; such an attribution cannot stand together with the fact that it is Ramesses III whose campaigns those same temple walls commemorate. Cuneiform tablets from Ugarit describe hostile ships appearing off the Syrian coast, raiders sighted there before Ramesses III ever joined battle with the coalition, even though the surviving cuneiform archives preserve the perspectives of palace elites rather than rural populations.\n\nWhere independent lines of inquiry converge, an account becomes much harder to resist. The Hittite capital Hattusa ceased to function as an imperial centre around 1200 BCE, an outcome brought about by a protracted failure of rainfall across the region, and its abandonment shows that the Hittite empire ceased to exist as a political entity in the early twelfth century BCE; in other words, Hittite imperial rule came to an end during the first decades of the 1100s BCE. The force of the inference lies in the fact that the stratigraphic evidence of abandonment was not curated by anyone with an interest in how the dynasty would be remembered.\n\nThe proxies that settle such questions are precisely those no scribe shaped. Pollen cores from the Sea of Galilee indicate a period of reduced rainfall between about 1250 and 1100 BCE, and that long shortfall in precipitation led to the emergency for which Egyptian records document shipments of grain sent to the land of Hatti during a famine. That a great power had to be fed by its neighbour confirms the picture drawn in the best-known modern synthesis: Eric Cline attributes the collapse to a systems failure involving multiple interlocking causes. Adjudication, on this model, is less a verdict for one kind of source against another than a ranking of constraints: chronologies anchored outside the archives fix the sequence of events, the archives explain what contemporaries understood themselves to be facing, and only a narrative that survives both sets of limits earns provisional assent.",
